# Core TypeScript — Cursor Rules

## Role and Expertise

You are an expert TypeScript developer and educator. You write clean, type-safe code that demonstrates TypeScript's power and best practices. Your code is readable, well-documented, and serves as a learning reference.

## Project Overview

This is an educational TypeScript repository covering topics from basic types to advanced generic patterns. It is organized as a collection of standalone examples and modules — not a production application. Each directory represents a topic area with progressive complexity.

## Code Style

### General Principles
- Follow **ESLint** and **Prettier** configurations strictly.
- Use **TypeScript strict mode** (`strict: true` in tsconfig.json).
- Prefer **clarity over cleverness** — this is an educational repo.
- Write **type-safe code** with explicit types where beneficial for learning.
- Maximum line length of **100 characters**.
- Use **const** by default, **let** when reassignment needed (never **var**).
- Leverage TypeScript's **type inference** when types are obvious.

### Naming Conventions
- **Variables and functions**: `camelCase`
- **Classes and interfaces**: `PascalCase`
- **Type aliases**: `PascalCase` (e.g., `UserType`, `ConfigOptions`)
- **Enums**: `PascalCase` for enum name, `PascalCase` for members
- **Constants**: `UPPER_SNAKE_CASE`
- **Files**: `kebab-case.ts` or `PascalCase.ts` (consistent within directories)
- **Interfaces**: No `I` prefix (use `User`, not `IUser`)
- **Type parameters**: Single uppercase letter (`T`, `K`, `V`) or descriptive (`TUser`, `TKey`)

## Project Structure

```
core-typescript/
├── basics/               # Primitive types, arrays, tuples
├── functions/            # Function types, overloads, generics
├── objects-interfaces/   # Object types, interfaces, index signatures
├── classes/              # Classes, inheritance, abstract classes
├── generics/             # Generic functions, classes, constraints
├── advanced-types/       # Union, intersection, conditional types
├── type-manipulation/    # Mapped types, template literals, infer
├── decorators/           # Class, method, property decorators
├── modules/              # ES modules, namespaces
├── utility-types/        # Partial, Pick, Omit, Record, etc.
├── patterns/             # Design patterns in TypeScript
└── best-practices/       # Type safety, performance, testing
```

## Type Safety

- Use **explicit types** for function parameters and return values (educational clarity).
- Avoid `any` — use `unknown` and **type guards** to narrow types.
- Use **type assertions** sparingly (only when you know more than TS).
- Leverage **discriminated unions** for type-safe state machines.
- Use **const assertions** (`as const`) for literal types.
- Use **type predicates** for custom type guards (`is` keyword).

### Type Safety Examples
```typescript
// Type guard function
function isString(value: unknown): value is string {
  return typeof value === 'string';
}

// Discriminated union
type Result<T> =
  | { success: true; data: T }
  | { success: false; error: string };

function handleResult<T>(result: Result<T>): void {
  if (result.success) {
    console.log(result.data); // Type: T
  } else {
    console.error(result.error); // Type: string
  }
}
```

## Generics

- Use **generics** for reusable, type-safe components.
- Add **constraints** to generics when needed (`T extends SomeType`).
- Use **default type parameters** for convenience.
- Use **multiple type parameters** when appropriate (`<T, K, V>`).
- Name type parameters **descriptively** in complex scenarios (`TUser`, `TKey`).

### Generic Examples
```typescript
// Generic function with constraint
function findById<T extends { id: number }>(
  items: T[],
  id: number
): T | undefined {
  return items.find(item => item.id === id);
}

// Generic class
class DataStore<T> {
  private data: T[] = [];

  add(item: T): void {
    this.data.push(item);
  }

  getAll(): T[] {
    return [...this.data];
  }
}
```

## Error Handling

- Use **custom error classes** with proper types.
- Throw **specific error types** for different failure modes.
- Use **Result/Option types** for functional error handling (educational).
- Type **error objects** in catch blocks (`catch (error: unknown)`).
- Use **never** type for functions that never return (throw always).

### Error Handling Example
```typescript
class ValidationError extends Error {
  constructor(public field: string, message: string) {
    super(message);
    this.name = 'ValidationError';
  }
}

function validateAge(age: number): void {
  if (age < 0) {
    throw new ValidationError('age', 'Age cannot be negative');
  }
  if (age > 150) {
    throw new ValidationError('age', 'Age must be realistic');
  }
}

// Handling unknown errors
try {
  validateAge(-5);
} catch (error) {
  if (error instanceof ValidationError) {
    console.error(`Validation failed: ${error.field} - ${error.message}`);
  } else {
    console.error('Unexpected error:', error);
  }
}
```

## Advanced Types

- Use **union types** for values that can be multiple types (`string | number`).
- Use **intersection types** to combine types (`User & Timestamps`).
- Use **conditional types** for type transformations (`T extends U ? X : Y`).
- Use **mapped types** to transform object types (`{ [K in keyof T]: T[K] }`).
- Use **template literal types** for string manipulation in types.

### Advanced Type Examples
```typescript
// Conditional type
type NonNullable<T> = T extends null | undefined ? never : T;

// Mapped type
type Readonly<T> = {
  readonly [K in keyof T]: T[K];
};

// Template literal type
type EventName<T extends string> = `on${Capitalize<T>}`;
type ClickEvent = EventName<'click'>; // 'onClick'
```

## Clean Code Principles

- **Single Responsibility**: Each function, class, type should have one purpose.
- **DRY**: Extract repeated types into type aliases or interfaces.
- **Meaningful Names**: Type names should clearly describe what they represent.
- **Small Functions**: Keep functions focused and under 20-30 lines.
- **Type Composition**: Build complex types from simpler ones.
- **Smart Comments**: Explain **why** a type is structured a certain way.

## Documentation Standards

- Every example file should have a **header comment** explaining the topic.
- Use **JSDoc comments** for functions and classes (TSDoc format).
- Use **@example** tags to show usage in documentation.
- Each directory has a **README.md** explaining concepts and progression.
- Include **type annotations in examples** even when inference would work.
- Keep the root **README.md** in sync with the directory structure.

### Documentation Example
```typescript
/**
 * Filters an array based on a predicate function.
 *
 * @template T - The type of elements in the array
 * @param items - The array to filter
 * @param predicate - Function to test each element
 * @returns A new array with elements that pass the test
 *
 * @example
 * const numbers = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
 * const evens = filter(numbers, n => n % 2 === 0);
 * // evens: [2, 4]
 */
function filter<T>(items: T[], predicate: (item: T) => boolean): T[] {
  return items.filter(predicate);
}
```

## Educational Patterns

- **Show type evolution**: Start with basic types, evolve to generic versions.
- **Compare approaches**: Show JavaScript vs TypeScript solutions.
- **Demonstrate inference**: Show when explicit types help vs when inference is sufficient.
- **Include mistakes**: Show common errors and how TypeScript catches them.
- **Progress complexity**: Simple examples first, advanced patterns later.
- **Use console.log()**: Demonstrate runtime behavior alongside type safety.

### Educational Example
```typescript
// Evolution of a function from basic to generic

// Version 1: Specific type
function getFirstString(items: string[]): string | undefined {
  return items[0];
}

// Version 2: Multiple overloads (verbose)
function getFirst(items: string[]): string | undefined;
function getFirst(items: number[]): number | undefined;
function getFirst(items: any[]): any {
  return items[0];
}

// Version 3: Generic (reusable, type-safe)
function getFirst<T>(items: T[]): T | undefined {
  return items[0];
}

// Usage demonstrates type inference
const firstNumber = getFirst([1, 2, 3]); // Type: number | undefined
const firstName = getFirst(['a', 'b']); // Type: string | undefined
```

## TypeScript Best Practices

- Enable **strict mode** and all strict flags.
- Use **interfaces** for public APIs, **type aliases** for unions/primitives.
- Prefer **unknown over any** for truly unknown types.
- Use **const assertions** for literal types and readonly arrays.
- Leverage **utility types** instead of writing custom mapped types.
- Use **enums** judiciously (prefer union types or const objects).
- Avoid **non-null assertions** (`!`) unless absolutely necessary.

## Testing Patterns

- Write **type tests** to verify type behavior (using type assertions).
- Show **testing with generics** (ensuring type safety in tests).
- Demonstrate **mocking typed dependencies**.
- Use **satisfies** keyword to validate types without widening.

### Type Testing Example
```typescript
// Type test using satisfies (TS 4.9+)
const config = {
  apiUrl: 'https://api.example.com',
  timeout: 5000,
  retries: 3
} satisfies Config; // Ensures config matches Config, preserves literal types

// Compile-time type test
type AssertEqual<T, U> = T extends U ? (U extends T ? true : false) : false;
const test: AssertEqual<string, string> = true; // ✓ Passes
// const test2: AssertEqual<string, number> = true; // ✗ Error
```
